// דברי חכמים — sayings gathered from the web (and from the app's own Talmud, Mishnah, Rambam and Tanchuma), reviewed
// one by one. Merged into src/data/divreiChachamim.mjs by scripts/leatzmi/build-divrei-chachamim.mjs.
//
// The owner's decision (2026-10): the words of Chazal and of the classic sages belong to everyone and may be taken from
// the web; what may not be copied is a modern edition's own work — its nikud, punctuation, notes, translation or
// arrangement. So every saying here is:
//   • the classic text's own words, unvocalized, cut as one whole contiguous passage (nothing paraphrased or joined);
//   • taken from a public-domain work (WEB_BOOKS[book].basis), through a named digital transcription (`digital`);
//   • stripped of the transcription's punctuation whenever that transcription is not itself public domain
//     (`punctuation`); where it is, only minimal sentence punctuation is kept; quotation marks and verse
//     references in brackets are always dropped;
//   • cited (`ref`), with a link to the exact place (`provenanceUrl`) and the date it was read (`retrieved`).
// Verification: every `source: 'sefaria'` saying was re-read from the live Sefaria segment of the named version and
// matched letter for letter (scripts/leatzmi/verify-divrei-chachamim-web.mjs repeats this); every `source: 'bundled'`
// saying is found, letter for letter, in the app's own bundled pack of that work — the build places it there (so the
// card opens it in the library) and tests/leatzmiDivreiChachamim.test.mjs checks it on every run.
// Selection: short, whole, gentle and guiding; no halachic technicality, nothing polemical; nothing that repeats a saying
// already in the collection. Sayings are grouped by book; the build keeps this order.

